        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart, and be no more stiffnecked&#x27; (Deu 10:16), Ephrem reads the heart-circumcision command as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of Paul&#x27;s &#x27;circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, and not in the letter&#x27; (Rom 2:29). The Deuteronomic call to interior circumcision is not a substitute for the bodily rite but its ground: the outward sign is empty without the inward reality. Tewahedo theology — uniquely among Christian communions still practising bodily circumcision per Jub 15:14-25 — cites Deu 10:16 as the canonical Old-Testament witness that BOTH circumcisions are required: the bodily sign that distinguishes the people and the heart-circumcision that perfects it.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 10:12-22. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Rom 2:29; Jub 15:14-25 (R.H. Charles, 1902 — PD).",
