        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On Moses&#x27; burial &#x27;in a valley in the land of Moab, over against Bethpeor: but no man knoweth of his sepulchre unto this day&#x27; (Deu 34:6), Ephrem reads the divinely-hidden grave as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of Jude 9&#x27;s account of the archangel Michael&#x27;s dispute with the devil over Moses&#x27; body — preserved in the Assumption of Moses tradition received by the Ethiopian canon. The hidden grave prepares Moses for his Transfiguration appearance with Elijah (Mt 17:3, Lk 9:30-31), suggesting a translation-pattern parallel to Enoch (Gen 5:24) and Elijah (2 Ki 2:11). Tewahedo Astə&#x27;arǝgya-Mussē (Translation of Moses) commemorates this hidden-grave tradition; Deu 34:6 + Jude 9 + the Buhe Transfiguration form the canonical triple-witness of Moses&#x27; translated-glorified state.</p></aside>',
